@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "5. Change the room, keep the player") {
        Games often replace the surroundings while keeping the player's score and identity. Build two rooms, switch between them, and learn which objects should belong to a level.

        This lesson uses the same entity, component, and SwiftUI ideas as the earlier lessons. Its complete code includes room artwork and buttons so you can concentrate on level loading.

        @Image(source: "spriteui-levels-02-preview.svg", alt: "A preview of the scene you will build in this lesson.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Give a room one root entity") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A room is an entity hierarchy: one root holds its backdrop, floor, and ball. The level coordinator can replace that whole hierarchy without replacing the world or the SwiftUI game view.

            `content.levels` supplies the coordinator. Its load closure prepares the next room and returns its root. The coordinator adds that root when the room is ready.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Run stage one. Watch the Garden ball bounce, then load Workshop and switch back. Each room gets a new ball. The room update count restarts because the room itself has been replaced.

                Find `levels.load` and the `return root` at the end of preparation. Those are the important boundaries; the drawing helper simply supplies a ball image without an asset file.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-levels-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-levels-01-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Notice that there is still one `GameView`. Loading a room changes its contents instead of constructing another game surface. In your own game, enemies and platforms can be children of the room root.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Keep progress outside the replaceable room") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The player should survive the room change. Create it separately, put its score in a component, and call `preserve(_:)` for it and the camera.

            The player and camera become persistent children of the world root. The room can be removed without removing either of them. This is a useful rule for deciding where game objects belong.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use stage two. Move the player and add score, then load the next room. Its ball restarts, but the player's ID, score, and position stay the same.

                Find the two preserve calls and the room's camera assignment. A fade makes the room change visible while keeping the same game world.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-levels-02.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-levels-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-levels-02-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Switch back and check the score again. Ask where each piece of state belongs: the room owns its ball; the persistent player owns progress. Saving the game later can follow the same distinction.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Handle a room that is not ready") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Loading real assets takes time and can fail. The final stage adds a short preparation wait, cancellation, and a test failure so you can see those cases before introducing real files.

            Preparation happens before the room is installed. Cancelling an animation after installation is different: the new room may already be the current one. The current-room readout shows which room the coordinator kept.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use the final stage. While a room is preparing, press Cancel: the current room stays. Enable the failure switch and try again: the current room stays and the message reports the error.

                Then try Fade and Crossfade. During crossfade, the outgoing room is an image while the incoming room runs. The transition covers the change; it does not run two worlds.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-levels-03.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-levels-03-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                You have reached the end of the course. You can describe game objects with components, move them with systems, connect player actions, check collisions, and organize levels.

                Choose your next task from <doc:rendering-and-resources>, <doc:saving-and-debugging>, or <doc:gameplay-and-movement>. You do not need to complete every advanced guide before continuing your game.
            }
        }
    }
}
